<section class="section how" id="how-it-works">
  <p class="section-label">How it works</p>
  <h2 class="section-headline">Register. Deploy. Monitor.</h2>
  <p class="section-sub">
    Three concepts, minimal configuration, and you pick the interface: Python, CLI, or Web UI.
  </p>

  <div class="grid">
    <article class="card">
      <p class="step">Step 1: Register</p>
      <h3>Immutable model registry</h3>
      <p class="body">
        Point dstk at your trained artifact. It gets versioned and stored immutably. No more "which
        pickle file was that?"
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block"><span class="t-fn">model</span> <span class="t-op">=</span> <span class="t-fn">registry</span><span class="t-op">.</span><span class="t-fn">register</span><span class="t-op">(</span>
  <span class="t-fn">name</span><span class="t-op">=</span><span class="t-str">"churn-predictor"</span><span class="t-op">,</span>
  <span class="t-fn">artifact</span><span class="t-op">=</span><span class="t-str">"./model.pkl"</span>
<span class="t-op">)</span></pre>
    </article>

    <article class="card">
      <p class="step">Step 2: Deploy</p>
      <h3>Painless, hardware agnostic</h3>
      <p class="body">
        dstk wraps your model in a container with a standard inference API. No Dockerfiles, no
        Kubernetes YAML, no tears.
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block"><span class="t-fn">Deployer</span><span class="t-op">().</span><span class="t-fn">deploy</span><span class="t-op">(</span><span class="t-fn">model</span><span class="t-op">)</span>

<span class="t-cm"># That's literally it</span></pre>
    </article>

    <article class="card full">
      <p class="step">Step 3: Monitor</p>
      <h3>Full audit trail, zero config</h3>
      <p class="body">
        Every deployment is tracked. See what's running, when it shipped, which version, and roll
        back if something goes wrong.
      </p>
      <div class="code-block split">
        <pre><span class="t-cm"># From Python</span>
<span class="t-fn">registry</span><span class="t-op">.</span><span class="t-fn">deployments</span><span class="t-op">(</span><span class="t-str">"churn-predictor"</span><span class="t-op">)</span>

<span class="t-out">[</span>
<span class="t-out">  {"{"} <span class="t-fn">version</span>: <span class="t-str">"1.2.0"</span>, <span class="t-fn">status</span>: <span class="t-str">"live"</span> {"}"},</span>
<span class="t-out">  {"{"} <span class="t-fn">version</span>: <span class="t-str">"1.1.0"</span>, <span class="t-fn">status</span>: <span class="t-str">"retired"</span> {"}"}</span>
<span class="t-out">]</span></pre>
        <pre><span class="t-cm"># Or from the CLI</span>
<span class="t-fn">$</span> <span class="t-fn">pydstk</span> deployments list churn-predictor

<span class="t-out">VERSION  STATUS   DEPLOYED</span>
<span class="t-out">1.2.0    <span class="t-success">live</span>     2 hours ago</span>
<span class="t-out">1.1.0    retired  3 days ago</span>
<span class="t-out">1.0.0    retired  2 weeks ago</span></pre>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
